# Native HTTP clients

Enable the generated HTTP surface and at least one backend:

```toml
[dependencies]
upwell = { version = "1", features = ["axum", "client", "reqwest"] }
```

Use `hyper` instead of or alongside `reqwest` when its constraints fit the deployment.

## Construct a Reqwest client

The committed HTTP fixture declares `GreetController`, so its generated `GreetControllerClient` is an inventory-backed example name.

<Example title="Call the fixture controller with Reqwest">

```rust
use upwell::axum::client::ReqwestClient;

let transport = ReqwestClient::new("https://api.example.test");
let client = GreetControllerClient::new(transport);
let response = client.greet_path(String::from("Ada")).await?;

println!("{}", response.body().message);
```

The exact generated method follows the committed controller handler (`greet_path`) and returns an <Symbol path="upwell_axum::client::response::HttpResponse" /> so status and headers remain available beside the decoded body.

</Example>

Use `ReqwestClient::with_client` to supply a configured `reqwest::Client` with deployment TLS, proxy, redirect, timeout, and pool policy. `set_header_provider` installs a native callback that returns fresh default headers for every request; call-specific headers still win.

## Construct a Hyper client

```rust
use upwell::axum::client::HyperClient;

let transport = HyperClient::new("http://127.0.0.1:3000");
let client = GreetControllerClient::new(transport);
```

The validated Hyper backend uses `hyper-util` pooling and is plain HTTP only. It enables no TLS stack. Terminate TLS at a trusted local proxy or use Reqwest when the client must connect directly to HTTPS.

## Add cross-cutting observation

Both backends store a typed <Symbol path="upwell_axum::client::interceptor::ClientInterceptor" />. `on_request` runs after the absolute URI and defaults are assembled, `on_response` runs when status and headers arrive before classification/decoding, and `on_error` observes terminal client errors.

An interceptor is observation/mutation around each call, not a retry policy. Retrying non-idempotent methods can duplicate remote work; implement retries only with an explicit operation policy and idempotency contract.

## Compare capabilities

| Capability | Reqwest | Hyper |
|---|---|---|
| Unary generated methods | yes | yes |
| Status/headers envelope | yes | yes |
| Streamed responses | yes | yes |
| Streamed requests | named by the validated capability docs | exercised by the committed HTTP fixture at the recorded source revision |
| Multipart body | encoded byte body | encoded byte body when method bounds fit |
| Direct HTTPS | through configured Reqwest | no TLS in validated backend |
| Attach a WebSocket request/reply transport | `with_websocket` | `with_websocket` |

The generated method's generic transport bounds are authoritative. The stored artifact is version-skewed: its `HttpClientStreaming` docs say Reqwest, but the committed source fixture at the artifact revision successfully calls a generated request-stream method through Hyper. Verify the exact packaged release's implementation and tests rather than treating either row as an unconditional 1.0 guarantee. If a backend lacks a capability in your build, use another backend rather than manually pretending the route is unary.

## Call generated streaming methods

The committed `Api` fixture emits the following exact method names and call shapes:

```rust plain
use futures::{StreamExt, stream};

let client = ApiClient::new(ReqwestClient::new("http://127.0.0.1:3000"));

// GET /api/ticks/{n}: outer call errors before streaming; items are u64.
let mut ticks = client.ticks(4).await?;
while let Some(tick) = ticks.next().await {
    println!("{tick}");
}

// POST /api/collect: generated NDJSON request stream, unary HttpResponse<u64>.
let total = client.collect(stream::iter([1_u64, 2, 3, 4])).await?;
assert_eq!(*total, 10);
```

These names are fixture-emitted, not reserved framework methods. A raw response wrapper similarly emits the declared controller method and yields `Bytes` chunks. The generated HTTP request-stream path is NDJSON-only in the committed macros; a raw byte request must use a supported ordinary body such as `Bytes` or a manually constructed backend request rather than a generated `#[stream]` method.

## Error and cancellation behavior

Non-success statuses declared by the generated contract become typed remote errors; unexpected statuses are classified through the backend and interceptor. Redirects can be surfaced as `ClientError::Redirect` depending on the supplied backend client's redirect policy. Decode and transport failures remain distinct.

Dropping a unary future stops local waiting, but the server may already be executing. Dropping a response stream closes local body consumption and should propagate a send failure/cancellation to a well-behaved producer. Configure transport and application deadlines explicitly; generated calls do not invent one universal timeout.

<Callout type="warning" title="Base URLs need scheme and authority">

Construct transports with a base such as `https://api.example.test`, not a route URL. Generated clients append their controller/route path and percent-encode path/query values.

</Callout>

## Troubleshoot native HTTP

- Unresolved client type: compile the defining controller crate with `client` and a backend feature.
- HTTPS fails under Hyper: use Reqwest or a TLS-terminating proxy.
- A streamed request does not compile on the selected backend: inspect that release's generated bounds and backend implementation; the committed artifact and source fixture disagree about Hyper coverage.
- Authentication is stale: return a fresh header map from the Reqwest header provider or use per-call headers.
- A route returns an unexpected status: inspect the full `HttpResponse`/`ClientError`, not only body decoding.

See <GuideRef slug="axum/http/streaming">streaming HTTP</GuideRef>, <GuideRef slug="axum/http/multipart-uploads">multipart uploads</GuideRef>, and <GuideRef slug="clients/native/websocket">native WebSocket clients</GuideRef>.
